1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,384
Bring the Moon twice as close to Earth, and one number explains almost everything that happens next. Eight.

2
00:00:07,384 --> 00:00:20,805
Tides come from the difference in the Moon's pull across our planet. That difference falls with the cube of distance. Halve the distance, and the force raising our oceans grows two cubed. Eight times stronger.

3
00:00:20,805 --> 00:00:29,277
The Moon would look twice as wide, covering four times as much sky. Full moon nights would be roughly four times brighter.

4
00:00:29,277 --> 00:00:36,597
Kepler's third law says it would speed up too. Instead of twenty seven days, one orbit would take under ten.

5
00:00:36,597 --> 00:00:47,010
Total solar eclipses would become common, and long. And the ring of fire would vanish forever, because the Moon could never look smaller than the Sun again.

6
00:00:47,010 --> 00:01:01,221
But the quietest change matters most. Tidal braking scales with the sixth power of distance. Two to the sixth is sixty four. Earth's spin would slow sixty four times faster, stretching our days far sooner.

7
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:05,983
One change. Real math. Follow for the next what if.

